course: language
lesson: 7
title: Kinds of sentences: statements, questions, commands, exclamations
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: English has four kinds of sentence: a statement tells (The cake is huge.), a question asks (What is that cake?), a command tells someone to do something (Eat the cake.), and an exclamation shows strong feeling (What a huge cake!).
explain: A command can be tiny: "Help!", "Run!" and "Stop!" are full sentences, because the "you" is understood; Mum's "Shoes on. Now." works as a command too, and "please" is kindness, not grammar.
explain: Don't judge by the first word: "What a huge cake!" and "What is that huge cake?" both start with what, but only one asks for an answer.
explain: Some sentences wear a disguise: "Would you mind closing the window?" looks like a question but is a polite request, so answering "No" and not moving makes the class laugh.
explain: Places have their own disguises too: in Irish English "Would you ever stop that racket?" means "Please stop that noise now", and in New Zealand and Australia "yeah nah" means a soft no.
fact: A statement tells something and usually ends with a full stop.
fact: A question asks for information and ends with a question mark.
fact: A command tells someone to do something; its verb form is called the imperative, and the "you" is understood, not said.
fact: One-word commands like "Help!", "Run!" and "Listen!" are complete sentences.
fact: An exclamation shows strong feeling and ends with an exclamation mark; in school grammar it often starts with "What" or "How", as in "What a huge cake!".
fact: In speech, raising your voice at the end (intonation) can turn a statement into a question: "You're coming tomorrow?"
fact: An indirect request is a polite way of asking someone to do something, like "Would you mind closing the window?"
fact: In Irish English "would you ever" is a polite but firm "please", and a "racket" is a loud noise.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand "yeah nah" usually means no, politely, and "nah yeah" usually means yes; listen to the last word.
fact: Signs leave words out, so we read them with common sense: "Dogs must be carried" means "if you have a dog, carry it".
fact: A rhetorical question or exclamation like "Isn't it a lovely day!" does not want an answer; it shows a feeling.
fact: In texts many people drop question marks; without one a message like "can u come over" can look flat or bossy.
exercise: What kind of sentence is "Shoes on. Now."? | A command | A question | A statement | An exclamation
exercise: Who is the understood subject of a command like "Sit down"? | you | I | they | nobody
exercise: Which is the question? | What is that huge cake? | What a huge cake! | That is a huge cake. | Eat that huge cake.
exercise: What kind of sentence is "What a huge cake!"? | An exclamation | A question | A command | A statement
exercise: Ruby's Kiwi cousin says "Yeah nah, I'm good" when offered more chips. What does she mean? | No thanks | Yes please | Maybe later | Give me all of them
exercise: Leon says "You're coming tomorrow?" with his voice going up. What makes it a question? | His voice rising at the end | The word "tomorrow" | The word "you're" | Nothing, it is a statement
exercise: A sign says "Dogs must be carried on the escalator." What does it mean? | If you have a dog, carry it on the escalator | Everyone must carry a dog with them on the escalator | Dogs must carry their owners | Only people with dogs can use the escalator
exercise: Mr Davies says "Would you mind closing the window?" What does he really want? | The window closed | A yes or no answer | To know if Ollie is cold | Ollie's opinion about windows
exercise: Skye's dad from Dublin says "Would you ever stop that racket?" What does he want? | Her to stop the noise now | To know if she will stop one day | Her to buy a tennis racket | Her to play louder
exercise: Is "Help!" a sentence? | Yes, a one-word command | No, sentences need three words | No, it has no verb | Only if you add "please"
exercise: Zainab texts "can u come over" with no question mark. What can happen? | It may look flat or bossy | It becomes illegal | It turns into a command by law | Nothing, punctuation never changes anything
exercise: Gran says "Isn't it a lovely day!" Does she want an answer? | No, she is showing she's pleased | Yes, she wants a yes or no | Yes, she wants the weather forecast | No, she is giving an order
exercise: Does a command need "please" to be a sentence? | No, please is kind but not needed for grammar | Yes, or it is not a proper sentence in grammar | Only in Britain | Only if it is written down
exercise: How many of these are commands? "Sit. Is it raining? What a mess! Close the door. I like pie." | Two | One | Three | Four
exercise: Which kind of sentence is "How tall you have grown!"? | An exclamation | A question | A command | A statement
exercise: Change "You are hungry." into a question without changing any word, only the punctuation. | You are hungry? | You are hungry! | You are hungry, | You are hungry.
exercise: An Aussie says "Nah yeah, let's go." What does he mean? | Yes, let's go | No, let's not go | He hasn't decided | He wants you to go alone
test: Your mate goes "'Run!' ain't a sentence, it's one word, mate." What's the best reply? | It's a full command; the "you" is understood | He's right, sentences need a subject written | He's right, sentences need five words | It's a noun, not a sentence
test: Your teacher asks "Would you like to tidy up now?" You say "Nah" and keep playing. What went wrong? | It was a polite request, not a real question | Nothing, you answered the teacher's question honestly | The teacher asked about the future | "Nah" is not an English word
test: A Gen Z cousin texts "omw" ("on my way") and her mum replies "is that a question?" Is it? | No, it's a statement telling her she's coming | Yes, every text message counts as a question | Yes, it asks if she should come | No, it's a command to come
test: Your Irish auntie says "Would you ever pass the salt, love?" What should you do? | Pass the salt | Say "maybe one day" | Explain where the salt is | Ask what "ever" means first
test: What would happen if English had no question marks or rising voices? | Questions and statements would be easy to mix up | Nothing, we'd still always know which ones were questions | Every sentence would become a command | Nobody could ask anything at all
test: A boomer neighbour reads "Shoes must be worn" at the pool shop and says "but I've got no shoes in my bag, bummer." ("Bummer" means what a pain.) What's the sensible reading? | People must wear shoes in the shop | You must carry spare shoes in a bag | Shoes are for sale and must be bought | Only shoes can enter the shop
test: In a game, an NPC (a computer character) says "What a brave hero you are!" A player says "it asked me a question." Is that right? | No, it's an exclamation showing feeling | Yes, it starts with "What" | Yes, it wants a yes or no | No, it's a command to be brave
test: Your Kiwi mate asks "Want to come to the beach? We'll grab jandals." You reply "Yeah nah." ("Jandals" are flip-flops.) What did you say? | No, softly | Yes, definitely | Bring jandals for me | Ask me again later
test: Lily writes "Can I have a biscuit." What is missing? | A question mark instead of the full stop | An exclamation mark straight after the word "I" | A capital B for biscuit | Nothing, it's correct
test: In a cartoon, a pirate growls "Walk the plank, ye scallywag." ("Ye" is old "you", a scallywag is a rascal.) What kind of sentence is it? | A command | A question | An exclamation of joy | A statement about planks
test: Your brother says "every sentence starting with 'what' is a question, easy." Which sentence proves him wrong? | What a mess! | What time is it? | What is your name? | What did you eat?
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an imperative sentence? | A sentence directing the addressee to perform an action, the subject being understood | A sentence soliciting information from the addressee | A sentence expressing an emotion without direction | A sentence recording a fact in the past tense
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the utterance "Would you mind closing the window?" does not ordinarily require the answer "yes" or "no". | It functions as a courteous request for the performance of an action | It constitutes a statement concerning the weather | It is grammatically defective and therefore meaningless | It is addressed to no particular person
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the expression "yeah nah" in Australian and New Zealand English. | It ordinarily signifies a courteous refusal | It ordinarily signifies an enthusiastic acceptance | It signifies an inability to hear the speaker | It is devoid of meaning in all contexts
exam: Given the notice "Dogs must be carried on the escalator", ascertain the interpretation most consistent with common sense. | Persons accompanied by a dog are required to carry it upon the escalator | Every person is required to carry a dog upon the escalator | Dogs are required to carry their owners | The escalator is reserved exclusively for dogs
